013,2014,2015,2016,2017,2018,2019,2020,2021,2022,2023,2024]}},"statement0":"<p style=\"margin-bottom: 12px;\">The word <span  \"><i>\"schizophrenic\" </i></span> is mentioned two or more times in <span style=\"color: rgba(255, 89, 98, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">0.0%</span> of neuroscience papers' Results sections, as of 2020-2024. </p>Among 2020-2024 papers containing <span  \"><i>\"schizophrenic\" </i></span>, <span style=\"color: rgba(94, 184, 242, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">37.3%</span> of significant p-values (p < .05) are fragile (.01 \u2264 p < .05) \u2013 i.e., barely fall under the standard significance threshold. This is similar to the typical neuroscience paper (median shown for reference). Relative to other words, <span  \"><i>\"schizophrenic\" </i></span> papers are in the top <span style=\"color: rgba(3, 152, 252, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">65<sup>th</sup></span> percentile in terms of fragile p-value percentage (lower = better). Note, p-values highly predict replicability (see <a href=\"../assets/pdf/Bogdan_2025_PsyChange_Manuscript_SuppMat.pdf\">the associated paper</a>).","statement1":"As of 2020-2024, papers containing <span  \"><i>\"schizophrenic\" </i></span> tend to be published in lower impact factor journals (<span style=\"color: rgba(105, 219, 99, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">2<sup>nd</sup></span> percentile among all words)  but tend to be cited more (<span style=\"color: rgba(255, 168, 82, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">58<sup>th</sup></span> percentile word). In general, this word is negatively associated with university ranking, such that higher-ranked institutions are less likely to publish papers containing <span  \"><i>\"schizophrenic\" </i></span> (<span style=\"color: rgba(170, 117, 250, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">39<sup>th</sup></span> percentile). "}